# Deterministic fuse check for the print-in-place toggle clamp.
# The whole mechanism legitimately prints as ONE watertight body (four moving
# parts, all captured), so printcheck cannot see a weld: if any of the 0.4 mm
# Z films or 0.25 mm XY films bridge during printing, the part still slices
# green and the clamp arrives frozen. fusecheck answers it on the sliced STL.
#
# No `flexure` line, deliberately: nothing in this mechanism is joined THROUGH
# a flexure — the moving parts are joined by pivot pins and films, and the arch
# spring only ever PUSHES on the lever's cam flank through a contact, never
# bridges material across it. So the raw shell count is the whole truth, and an
# ignore-zone would only hide things (round 1's over-large arch AABB deleted
# the nub weld it existed to ignore, masking a real defect — see NOTES.md).
#
# The sliced part must show its true shell count: base, carrier, lever, link,
# plus the three trapped pin heads (head cones float one pip_z film above what
# they retain) = 7. Any welded film merges two shells and drops the count.
assert  over-center-toggle-clamp.stl 7

# Mandatory negative control: `part="fused"` zeroes every print-in-place
# clearance and makes the cam carve deliberately interfere 0.3 mm, reproducing
# the weld as one solid. It must read exactly 1 body — proving the check fires.
control fused 1
